{"arrivo":"fr","origine":"cel-gau","origine_nome":"Gaulish","n":34,"n_alternative":13,"parole":[{"parola":"auvent","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"awning, canopy (over entrance)","termine_origine":"*andebanno","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*andebanno","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*an-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-pro","ambiti":["arte"],"via":"","testo":"Inherited from Middle French auvans, from Old French anvant (1180), from Gaulish *andebanno (“eaves, portico”) (compare Occitan ambans, Catalan envà), compound of ande (“intensive prefix”) (compare Welsh an-) + banno (“horn”) (compare Welsh ban), ultimately from Proto-Celtic *an- + *bandā."},{"parola":"balle","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":3,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"chaff (inedible casing of a grain seed)","termine_origine":"*balu","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*balu","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From Gaulish *balu."},{"parola":"balme","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"cavern or rock shelter","termine_origine":"*balma","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*balma","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Inherited from Middle French baume, baulme, from Gaulish *balma"},{"parola":"bief","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"reach (of water)","termine_origine":"*bedom","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*bedom","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*bedom","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*bʰedʰ-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From Old French bied, bié, from Gaulish *bedom, from Proto-Celtic *bedom (“something that is dug”), ultimately from the root Proto-Indo-European *bʰedʰ-."},{"parola":"bonde","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"a plug, a bung","termine_origine":"*bunda","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*bunda","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*bʰudʰ-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":["sport"],"via":"","testo":"Ultimately from Gaulish *bunda, feminine form of *bundos (“bottom”), from Proto-Indo-European *bʰudʰ-, *bʰudʰmḗn. Cognate with Piedmontese bondon."},{"parola":"boue","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"mud; dirt","termine_origine":"*bawā","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*bawā","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Gaulish *bawā."},{"parola":"bourbe","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"mire","termine_origine":"*borwa","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*borwa","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From a proposed Gaulish *borwa (“hot springs”), related to Gaulish Borvo (“Celtic god of hot springs”)."},{"parola":"bouse","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"cowpat","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"xlg","nome":"Ancient Ligurian","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"xlg","ambiti":["diritto_politica"],"via":"","testo":"From Gaulish or Ancient Ligurian. Cognate with Piedmontese busa, Ligurian bêusa, bûsa, bûsia and Occitan bosa, bosia."},{"parola":"brasser","pos":"verb","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"to brew (beer)","termine_origine":"*brakis","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*brakis","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*mrakis","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*merk-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":["cucina"],"via":"","testo":"From Old French bracier, from brace (“malt”), from Gaulish *brakis (Latinized accusative bracem in Pliny) (compare Scottish Gaelic braich, Welsh brag), from Proto-Celtic *mrakis, from Proto-Indo-European *merk- (“to be soaked”) (compare Latin marceō (“to wither, droop”), North Frisian marig (“tallow"},{"parola":"brie","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"brie","termine_origine":"*briga","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*briga","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":["cucina"],"via":"","testo":"Named after Brie, France, from Gaulish *briga (“hill”). Doublet of Bourgogne."},{"parola":"brin","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Unknown; Possibly; perhaps","significato":"blade (of grass)","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*brinikā","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*bʰrugh-no-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Unknown origin; possibly of Gaulish origin (compare Catalan bri, Spanish brenca (“fiber”), brinza (“blade of grass, filament”)), from Proto-Celtic *brinikā, from *brinos (“filament, fiber”) (compare Breton broenenn, Welsh brwynen), from Proto-Indo-European *bʰrugh-no- (“twig”), perhaps related to th"},{"parola":"dru","pos":"adj|adv","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"thick","termine_origine":"*drudos","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*drudos","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Gaulish *drudos (“strong, vigorous”)."},{"parola":"gober","pos":"verb","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"to swallow whole","termine_origine":"*gobbos","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*gobbos","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*gobbos","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*ǵoph₃o-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Gaulish *gobbos, from Proto-Celtic *gobbos (“muzzle, snout, beak”), from Proto-Indo-European *ǵoph₃o- (“eat, masticate (of animals)”). Compare Irish gob, Scottish Gaelic gob (“beak, bill”) and English gob."},{"parola":"gord","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"crawl, a stake enclosure at a body of water to catch fish","termine_origine":"*gorton","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*gorton","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Gaulish *gorton ‘hedge, enclosure’; cf. Irish gort, Cornish gorth, Welsh garth."},{"parola":"jachère","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"fallow; fallow land (especially when left uncultivated on purpose)","termine_origine":"*gansco","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*gansco","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*kak-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":["agricoltura"],"via":"","testo":"First attested in 1690. Formerly ‘tillage, land prepared for plowing’, from Old French gaschiere, gaskiere, from Gaulish *gansco (“branch”) (compare Old Irish gesca); from Proto-Indo-European *kak-. Akin to Spanish and Portuguese gancho (“hook”)."},{"parola":"lotte","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Probably; perhaps; obscure","significato":"burbot (a freshwater fish, Lota lota)","termine_origine":"*lotta","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*lotta","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":["cucina"],"via":"","testo":"Probably of pre-Roman origin, perhaps from Gaulish *lotta, which would explain the preserved -t in French. Of obscure ultimate origin, with no known Celtic cognates of the Gaulish word."},{"parola":"marne","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"marl (mixed earthy substance)","termine_origine":"*margila","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*margila","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Inherited from Old French marne, variant of marle, from Gaulish *margila, a diminutive of marga, attested in Pliny."},{"parola":"sagne","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"synonym of tourbière (“bog”)","termine_origine":"*sagna","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*sagna","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From Gaulish *sagna, compare Latin stāgnum."},{"parola":"talus","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Unknown; Possibly","significato":"slope, embankment","termine_origine":"*talutum","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*talutum","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Unknown. Possibly from Gaulish *talutum (“slope”), derived from *talu (“front”), from Proto-Celtic *talu (“front”). Compare Latin talutium (“slope containing gold deposits”)."},{"parola":"trogne","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"pollard (pollarded tree)","termine_origine":"trugna","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"trugna","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*srognā","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Gaulish trugna (“nose, snout”), which could be related to *srogna (“nose”), from Proto-Celtic *srognā. Cognate with Welsh trwyn, Cornish tron, Breton stroen."},{"parola":"vanneau","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"lapwing (bird)","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*wesnālā","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Gaulish, from Proto-Celtic *wesnālā. Cognate with Old Irish fannall (Irish fáinleog, Scottish Gaelic ainleag) and Welsh gwennol."},{"parola":"beignet","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"alternatively","significato":"beignet (fritter filled with fruit etc)","termine_origine":"*bunia","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*bunia","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"frk","nome":"Frankish","termine":"*bungjō","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"gem-pro","nome":"Proto-Germanic","termine":"*bungô","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*bʰenǵʰ-","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*bunia","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Inherited from Middle French bignet, from Old French bignet (“fried dough enveloping a food substance”), a diminutive of bigne, bugne, buyne (“lump, swelling”), from Frankish *bungjō (“lump, bump, swelling”), from Proto-Germanic *bungô, *bunkô (“lump, heap, crowd”), from Proto-Indo-European *bʰenǵʰ-"},{"parola":"blaireau","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Possibly; alternatively","significato":"badger","termine_origine":"*blaros","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*blaros","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"frk","nome":"Frankish","termine":"*blari","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*blaros","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*blāwos","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Inherited from Middle French blereau, blariau, from Old French blarel, from blair. * Possibly from Frankish *blari (“sporting a white blaze on the forehead”) (compare *blasā). * Alternatively, from Gaulish *blaros, referring to the color gray, closely related to Proto-Celtic *blāwos (“yellow”). * Ac"},{"parola":"creux","pos":"adj","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Uncertain; Possibly; alternatively; modello unc/uncertain","significato":"hollow","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"la-lat","nome":"Late Latin","termine":"crosuin","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"corrosum","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"la-vul","nome":"Vulgar Latin","termine":"","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Uncertain. Possibly from Late Latin crosuin, from Latin corrosum, or alternatively from a Vulgar Latin *crosus, of Gaulish origin."},{"parola":"encombrer","pos":"verb","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Possibly; alternatively","significato":"to block off, to clutter, to clutter up, to congest","termine_origine":"*komberū","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*komberū","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"la-eme","nome":"Early Medieval Latin","termine":"incombrō","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*komberū","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"cel-pro","nome":"Proto-Celtic","termine":"*kombereti","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*bʰéreti","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"cumulus","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*ḱewh₁-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Inherited from Middle French encombrer, from Old French encombrer (“to hinder, burden, encumber”), from Early Medieval Latin incombrāre (“to hinder, inconvenience, burden”), from combrus (“barricade of felled trees”), possibly from Gaulish *komberū (cf. Breton kember, Irish comar, cumar, Welsh cymme"},{"parola":"glaise","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Uncertain; Possibly; perhaps; alternatively; obscure","significato":"clay","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"gem","nome":"Germanic","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"gmw-pro","nome":"Proto-West Germanic","termine":"*klaij","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"glis","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Inherited from Middle French glaise, glase, from Old French glaise, gloise, of obscure and uncertain origin. According to one theory, derived from Gaulish *glisa, which is attested in Late Latin glissomarga, glīsomarga (“a kind of marl, white marl”), although the precise meaning of *gliso- is uncert"},{"parola":"loche","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Unknown; perhaps; alternatively","significato":"one of the several species of giant slugs belonging to the families Arionidae and Limacidae","termine_origine":"*leuka","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*leuka","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":["scienza"],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"la-vul","nome":"Vulgar Latin","termine":"*laukka","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*leuka","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"frk","nome":"Frankish","termine":"*loppijā","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"gem-pro","nome":"Proto-Germanic","termine":"*lubbǭ","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"*lewbʰ-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Inherited from Middle French loche, from Old French loche, further origin unknown: * Perhaps from Vulgar Latin *laukka (“loach”), which could be from Gaulish *leuka (“loach, slug”), also attested as the feminine name Leuca, from leux (“bright, light”), a reference to slugs' bright appearance, the fi"},{"parola":"losange","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"perhaps; alternatively; obscure","significato":"rhombus","termine_origine":"*lausā","catena":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*lausā","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"cel-gau","ambiti":["diritto_politica"],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"cel-gau","nome":"Gaulish","termine":"*lausā","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"From Middle French losange, losenge, from Old French losenge (“a drawn shape on fabric, rhombus”), of obscure origin. Perhaps ultimately from Gaulish *lausā (“flat stone, stone slab”). Alternatively, perhaps from earlier Old French losenge (“flattery, deceitful praise”), from t